[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged as one issue and marked known. Read the reports, not the
subject lines, and it holds three problems with three different fixes. Each now has its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix closes only one of them. The other two keep coming in after it ships.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} issues={{ parser: `${ISSUES}/1`, hint: `${ISSUES}/2`, shift: `${ISSUES}/3` }} />

"Tagged as" is what intake guessed from the reporter's browser. It disagrees with the problem in
both directions, so it is not a way to sort these.

## The three problems

**`parser` — the shared parser reads day-first dates month first.** A tenant whose locale writes
the day first books 7 April and the invite says 4 July. Any channel, every tenant on file. This is
the bug #4471 names, and the fix queued for it.

**`hint` — booking-v3 tells people to type month first, then reads the date day first.** The date
field prints `MM/DD/YYYY`. People type what it says; the form reads the tenant's way. Only reports
through the form, only tenants on booking-v3. The result looks like the parser bug (a flipped
month), so subject matching put it in #4471. A parser fix does not touch the hint.

**`shift` — bookings move by one day.** These reports flip nothing: Tuesday 12 May becomes Monday
11 May, a client in Lisbon and a client in Toronto get different days for one meeting, an invite
arrives before its booking, a month where every booking is one day off. No day/month reading
produces that. It points at time zones (conversion to UTC, or a DST boundary), not at date order.
The desk had already noted these as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`), but counted them under
`parser` because of their tenant.

## How they were split

1. One id, one report: the export pages overlap, so repeats are dropped first.
2. Reports the desk read as a one-day move are `shift`.
3. The rest go by tenant and way in (`causes.ts`): a day-first tenant's report through
   booking-v3's form is `hint`; any other is `parser`.

Subject lines and intake tags play no part. The same subject turns up under all three problems.

## For the desk

- Matching on subject merged three bugs into one known ticket
  ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)). Before you merge into a big ticket,
  read what the report shows.
- When a report does not fit the ticket's bug, it gets a ticket of its own, not a footnote.

